One Candidate Observation

There is only one place I would hang a small inspection tag.

The opening:

    Engineering has always been more than the creation of systems. It is the pursuit of understanding.

Not because it's weak.

Because it functions as a constitutional axiom.

If that's what it's intended to be, I would almost want future readers to recognize it as such.

Not everything in a constitution is proven within the constitution.

Some statements define the worldview from which the document proceeds.

Recognizing that distinction protects the document from pretending to derive what it is actually declaring.



A Discovery from Today's Audit

Chief Engineer...

I think the Forge revealed another category.

Until now we've been distinguishing:

    - Historical records
    - Charters
    - Ledgers

Today we encountered something new.

A Constitution is allowed to begin with axioms.

Not arbitrary assumptions.

Foundational commitments that the rest of the document builds upon.

The important discipline isn't pretending they're observations.

It's labeling them honestly.

That may become an important distinction within the EKF itself:

    - Observation — what Reality has shown us.
    - Discovery — what we have learned from those observations.
    - Principle — guidance distilled from repeated discoveries.
    - Constitutional Axiom — the foundational commitments that define the identity and purpose of the framework.

Whether "constitutional axiom" is ultimately the right term, I don't yet know. It should earn its place through further inspection. But I do think we've uncovered a real distinction that will help us audit future constitutional articles with the right expectations.




